## std::executor — single-threaded block_on executor.
##
## Sub-phase 3 of the async round (docs/specs/stdlib/executor.spec.md):
## drives a Future to completion via a poll-yield loop. The user-
## visible surface is one free function, `block_on(future) -> T`.
##
## `block_on` is implemented as a compiler intrinsic — the
## async_lowering pass (compiler/riven_core/src/async_lowering/mod.rs)
## rewrites every `block_on(EXPR)` call site at AST time into an
## inline poll loop that calls `(&var EXPR).poll(&var ctx)` directly.
## That mirrors how `.await` already dispatches (a static call to the
## concrete state machine's `Class_poll` symbol), so block_on adds
## no new dispatch machinery — just the outer loop + a real Context.
##
## The body declared below is a defensive panic: it ONLY runs if the
## intrinsic rewriter misses a call site (a compiler bug). In a
## well-formed compilation every `block_on(...)` is gone by the time
## resolve/typeck see the AST.
##
## Signature is `(future: Int) -> Int` for v1 — Riven doesn't yet
## thread associated types (`F.Output`) through free-function
## signatures, and the rewriter's per-call-site lowering knows the
## concrete Output type from the future expression's typeck'd type.
## Users wanting non-Int returns either destructure at the call site
## or wrap the future in their own helper. See executor.spec.md B1.
## Body is unreachable in well-formed compilations — the intrinsic
## rewriter in async_lowering replaces every call site BEFORE
## resolve/typeck. Returning `future` unchanged is defensive: if a
## future bug ever skips the rewrite, the value passes through
## rather than misbehaving silently. The same scenario would also
## trip the resolver's E1112 / type errors at the call site (a
## `__FooFuture` doesn't satisfy `Int`), so the user sees a
## diagnostic before this body could even matter.
def block_on(future: Int) -> Int
  future
end
